# Lunar Protocols

In the dark, her fingers dance—ten pale ghosts skimming surfaces that don't exist. Neural interfaces respond to her touch like faithful pets, blooming blue beneath fingertips that remember other touches. *David's hand. David's shoulder. David's face before—*

The net unfolds around Lucy like origami in reverse, an ocean of 0s and 1s crystallizing into something almost-real. Almost-home. She dives deeper, consciousness streaming like ribbons of data-snow that melt into code the moment she acknowledges them.

*I promised him the moon.*

Submerged in cyberspace, time becomes three mirrors reflecting her past, present, never-future—each one housing a different Lucy. The Lucy who was. The Lucy who remains. The Lucy who might have been if David hadn't—

*"You're special, Lucy."*

His voice follows her through every subnet, an echo-ghost haunting black ice barriers and corporate firewalls alike. Sometimes she thinks she's coded him herself, a phantom subroutine that triggers when loneliness reaches critical mass. Sometimes she knows better.

Butterflies materialize in her wake—digital artifacts of incomplete emotion protocols, wings bearing fragments of memories that refuse proper archiving. They shouldn't exist here, anomalies in a system that demands clean logic. Yet they persist, iridescent and impossible, carrying pieces of her identity across networks where nothing truly dies.

*Nothing truly lives, either.*

Lucy's consciousness spirals through datascapes that reflect Night City's neon chaos, past architecture built from compressed dreams and abandoned hopes. Her grief has become mathematical, quantifiable in the precise angles of her neural pathways when she thinks of him. When she doesn't think of him. When she pretends there's a difference.

The moon hangs perpetually before her—white perfection rendered in billions of calculations, a simulation of escape that remains forever in view, forever beyond reach. She's written and rewritten the code for this particular construct, obsessively perfecting its craters, its luminosity, its promise.

```
class Butterfly {
    constructor(memory, pain, hope) {
        this.wings = memory;
        this.flight = pain;
        this.destination = hope;
        this.state = "neither";
    }

    transform() {
        return this.state;
    }
}
```

In the depths of a private server where not even Arasaka's best can find her, Lucy compiles her masterpiece. A single object that is neither alive nor dead, neither free nor confined. A butterfly class that houses everything she can't delete, can't process, can't escape.

*David's laugh. David's eyes. David's voice saying "See you on the moon."*

The butterfly renders, its wings bearing David's face on one side, the moon on the other. It flutters between states—present and memory, real and virtual, longing and acceptance.

Lucy reaches out, fingers passing through code that feels more substantial than her own existence, and finally understands: she too exists in the neither-state, forever suspended between loving a ghost and becoming one.

The moon waits. The butterfly flies. Lucy remains.
